<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.15.3: Using Direct Variation to Solve Application Problems, Part 1</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00333</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.15.3: Using Direct Variation to Solve Application Problems, Part 1</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d81f20aa-c758-40a7-b581-ac5de18df0f5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Return to your original group and work through this next part of your assigned problem. Then be ready to share.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">When Raoul runs on the treadmill at the gym, the number of calories, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, he burns varies directly with the number of minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, he uses the treadmill. He burned 315 calories when he used the treadmill for 18 minutes. </para>
          <para id="para-00003">How many calories would he burn if he ran on the treadmill for 25 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>437.5</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, so Raoul would burn 437.5 calories if he used the treadmill for 25 minutes.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">The number of calories, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, burned varies directly with the amount of time, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, spent exercising. Arnold burned 312 calories in 65 minutes exercising. </para>
          <para id="para-00007">How many calories would he burn if he exercises for 90 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>432</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para></solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">The distance a moving body travels, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, varies directly with time, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, it moves. A train travels 100 miles in 2 hours.</para>
          <para id="para-00011">How many miles would it travel in 5 hours?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013">250 miles</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
        <exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00004">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00014">For example 1, how could you find out how many minutes Raoul would need to use the treadmill to burn a certain number of calories?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. Substitute the number of calories you want to burn into the equation for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and then solve for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Video: Using Direct Variation to Solve Application Problems</title>
      <para id="para-00016">Watch the following video to learn more about how to use direct variation to solve an application problem.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/262332920b652e0ac822f2af48b9d1dc5c3c6baf"/>
      </media>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00017">Sophia is making parts for a model ship. The number of parts, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>p</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, she can make in a day varies directly with the number of hours, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>h</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, she works. She can make 28 parts in 4 hours. How many parts would Sophia make in 9 hours?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00005">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00006">
        <title>Solve Application Problems Using Direct Variation</title>
        <para id="para-00018">In applications using direct variation, generally we will know values of one pair of the variables and will be asked to find the equation that relates <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Then we can use that equation to find values of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> for other values of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        <para id="para-00019">Here’s how to solve applications of direct variation problems.</para>
        <note id="note-00002">
          <para id="para-00020"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation for direct variation. Identify the variables.</para>
        <para id="para-00021"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the given values for the variables.</para>
        <para id="para-00022"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for the constant of variation.</para>
        <para id="para-00023"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation that relates <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> using the constant of variation.</para>
        <para id="para-00024"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the value for the variable.</para>
        <para id="para-00025"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Solve.</para>
        </note>
        <para id="para-00026">Let’s look at an application problem:</para>
        <para id="para-00027">If the cost of a pizza varies directly with its diameter, and an 8-inch pizza costs $12, how much would a 6-inch pizza cost?</para>
        <para id="para-00028"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation for direct variation. Identify the variables. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00029"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: cost of pizza, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: diameter</para>
        <para id="para-00030"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the given values for the variables. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00031"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for the constant of variation. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation that relates x and y using the constant of variation. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the value for the variable. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00034"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Solve. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        <para id="para-00035">What does this mean in the context of the problem?</para>
        <para id="para-00036">A pizza that has a diameter of 6 inches costs $9.</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00003">
          <title> Try It: Solve Application Problems Using Direct Variation</title>
            <para id="para-00037">The number of buckets of water, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, filled by a spring varies directly with the number of minutes, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, passed. The spring fills 3 buckets of water in 14 minutes.</para>
              <exercise id="exer-00005">
            <problem id="prob-00005">
              <para id="para-00038">How many buckets would be filled in 42 minutes?</para>
              <para id="para-00039">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00005">
              <para id="para-00040">Compare your answer: Here is how to model this application problem of direct variation.</para>
              <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the equation for direct variation. Identify the variables. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00042"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: number of buckets, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>: number of minutes</para>
              <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Substitute the given values for the variables. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Solve for the constant of variation. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>k</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mo>/</m:mo><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00045"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Write the equation that relates <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> using the constant of variation. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>m</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the value for the variable. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>14</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>42</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
              <para id="para-00047"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Solve. <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> buckets</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise>
        </note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>